::heading level=2 id=outside
What must watch from outside
::end

::paragraph id=outside-why
Two suites cannot run inside the sandbox, by their own nature. The attack payloads prove the kernel boundary by standing outside it and watching real escapes fail — run them confined and the outer wall fails their positive controls. And doc-shot drives a real Chromium, which does not launch inside the boundary. The full workspace suite includes both, plus the doc-run and doc-cli tests that execute blocks (a sandbox does not nest). They stay a host-shell command:
::end

::code id=outside-commands lang=bash
export PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH"        # rustup ahead of any Homebrew cargo on this machine
cd rust
cargo test                                  # the whole suite, attacks and Chromium included
cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo fmt --check
./editor/build.sh                           # after touching doc-core: rebuild both wasm engines
::end

::heading level=2 id=driving
Driving the rendered surfaces
::end

::paragraph id=driving-note
`dx play` is a read with hands: it loads the same script-free render every screenshot uses, performs scripted input, and returns stamped frames — nothing the document says executes. These are the standing checks; each is a command, and what it must show is stated beside it.
::end

::code id=driving-commands lang=bash
dx play examples/GETTING_STARTED.dx --script "wait 400ms; scroll 1600; wait 400ms"   # the framed site scrolls inside its sandboxed view
dx png examples/example_site_plan.dx --block plan --out /tmp/board.png               # a board at natural size, no node clipped
dx play examples/showcase.dx --script "wait 300ms; scroll 800; wait 300ms"           # stamped frames of the page under input
::end

::checklist id=driving-seen
[x] The site-plan board renders every node at its stated box, edges legible
[x] The getting-started view frames the shipped site and scrolls inside its frame
[x] Play returns frames stamped with their moment and action
[x] DX.app rebuilt from this tree; `dx doctor` reports every surface current
::end
